WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.029 --> 00:00:07.500
What do you know about Argentina?

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:11.400
Perhaps its food: wine, meat, dulce de leche.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:15.709
Or its culture: the tango, Borges, the city
of Buenos Aires.

00:00:15.709 --> 00:00:22.099
But there’s a part of Argentina that you
probably know very little about.

00:00:22.099 --> 00:00:30.860
From 1976 to 1983, Argentina was ruled by
one of the most BRUTAL dictatorships in the

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:32.760
history of Latin America.

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:39.329
It was a regime, supported and funded by the
United States, that engaged in one of the

00:00:39.329 --> 00:00:48.190
most violent and ruthless campaigns of extermination
in the entire twentieth century.

00:00:48.190 --> 00:00:55.940
Argentina had been marked by political instability
since 1955, when the military overthrew President

00:00:55.940 --> 00:01:01.739
Juan Perón and banned his party from taking
part in national elections.

00:01:01.739 --> 00:01:04.729
In the meantime, left-wing guerrillas emerged.

00:01:04.729 --> 00:01:10.320
Perón returned to the country in 1973, and
was reelected president.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:15.979
When he died the next year, his wife, Isabel,
took power.

00:01:15.979 --> 00:01:21.840
Amidst an economic crisis, the government
supported right-wing paramilitary groups in

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:24.940
order to fight the guerrilla movement.

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:28.100
Political violence became a top concern.

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:35.580
Right after midnight on March 24th, 1976,
the Argentine military arrested Isabel Perón,

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:43.320
occupied radio and TV stations, and released
their first statement: the country was now

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:45.159
under military rule.

00:01:45.159 --> 00:01:49.310
They promised to restore the country to order
and end the violence.

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:54.890
They even called the new government “the
National Reorganization Process.”

00:01:54.890 --> 00:01:55.890
Some believed in that promise.

00:01:55.890 --> 00:02:03.690
The American ambassador called it “the best
executed and most civilized coup in Argentine

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:04.829
history.”

00:02:04.829 --> 00:02:10.551
To the American ambassador, the coup’s leader—a
general named JORGE RAFAEL VIDELA—was a

00:02:10.551 --> 00:02:16.409
“moderate” who could “defeat the left-wing
terrorists.”

00:02:16.409 --> 00:02:24.990
After all, the generals who executed the coup
were trained by the U.S. military at the School

00:02:24.990 --> 00:02:27.069
of the Americas.

00:02:27.069 --> 00:02:29.860
But they had something bigger in mind.

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:37.120
Beecause, in the end, they had come to do
way more than fight the guerrillas, whom they

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:39.650
crushed within a few months.

00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:47.590
For them, “terrorism” meant anything that
went against their vision of a pure society.

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:50.769
Many of them harbored fascist sympathies.

00:02:50.769 --> 00:02:58.590
Videla himself declared: “a terrorist is
not only someone with a weapon or a bomb,

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:07.430
but anyone who spreads ideas which are contrary
to our Western and Christian civilization.”

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:12.069
So the generals sought to “disappear”—kidnap,
imprison, torture, kill—everyone they saw

00:03:12.069 --> 00:03:13.980
as a threat.

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:23.220
That meant not only those involved in resistance
activities, but also trade unionists, Jews,

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:29.810
writers, homosexuals, even priests and nuns
who worked among Argentina’s poor.

00:03:29.810 --> 00:03:37.210
This was their opportunity to “PURIFY”
Argentine society—by force.

00:03:37.210 --> 00:03:45.760
The military called this repression La Guerra
Sucia: “the Dirty War,” a war against

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:52.040
a “hidden” enemy in which ANY means were
justified.

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:57.760
Argentina became a theater in a regional terror
campaign called “OPERATION CONDOR.”

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:04.930
Right-wing dictatorships across Latin America—not
just Argentina but also Chile, Paraguay, Uruguay,

00:04:04.930 --> 00:04:12.159
and Brazil—pursued a RELENTLESS CAMPAIGN
of torturing and murdering leftists.

00:04:12.159 --> 00:04:19.890
These regimes murdered tens of thousands of
dissidents in the most brutal fashions imaginable.

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:24.810
And it was all backed, even funded, by the
United States.

00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:32.810
In fact, in 1976, American Secretary of State
Henry Kissinger met with the new regime’s

00:04:32.810 --> 00:04:34.460
foreign minister.

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:40.560
The Argentine minister thought that Kissinger
would lecture him about the military’s violence.

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:43.830
But Kissinger did not raise the subject.

00:04:43.830 --> 00:04:50.120
When the minister asked, Kissinger responded
that they should simply “clean up the problem.”

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:57.030
According to the State Department, the Argentines
saw Kissinger’s words as a “green light”

00:04:57.030 --> 00:04:59.430
to do whatever they pleased.

00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:04.520
After the meeting with Kissinger, the repression
accelerated.

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:12.550
The Argentine military built clandestine detention
camps across the entire country to house political

00:05:12.550 --> 00:05:13.550
prisoners.

00:05:13.550 --> 00:05:19.580
There, prisoners were tortured to obtain information
about other political activists.

00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:28.000
The torture techniques employed by the military
were brutal: rapes, electric prods, beatings.

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:34.290
After torture, prisoners were killed, and
their bodies were burned or buried in mass

00:05:34.290 --> 00:05:35.830
graves.

00:05:35.830 --> 00:05:43.070
The Argentine military soon became famous
for their so-called “death flights,” where

00:05:43.070 --> 00:05:50.800
prisoners were boarded on a plane, stripped
naked, and dropped over the ocean, with their

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:57.840
bodies sometimes washing up on the beaches
around Buenos Aires.

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:00.569
Even pregnant women were tortured.

00:06:00.569 --> 00:06:08.820
In many cases, the military executed them
after giving birth, stole their children,

00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:11.030
and gave them to military families.

00:06:11.030 --> 00:06:18.620
At the same time, the Argentine military government
implemented a harsh system of “economic

00:06:18.620 --> 00:06:19.620
reform.”

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:25.260
The program was received with praise from
Wall Street: the president of Chase Bank,

00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:31.680
David Rockefeller, called it “brilliant,
solid and absolutely realistic.”

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:37.910
Rockefeller was a personal friend of the regime’s
Minister of Economy, Martínez de Hoz.

00:06:37.910 --> 00:06:44.080
Argentina abolished price controls, froze
wages, liberalized the financial markets,

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:47.270
and ended industrial protectionist measures.

00:06:47.270 --> 00:06:51.680
It was a rapid neoliberal transformation of
the economy.

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:55.710
And the consequences of that reform were disastrous.

00:06:55.710 --> 00:07:02.699
In one year, the real wages of Argentine workers
fell 30 percent.

00:07:02.699 --> 00:07:08.330
Argentine industry went into a deep crisis,
and living standards plummeted.

00:07:08.330 --> 00:07:14.541
One famous journalist, Rodolfo Walsh, wrote
of the effect of these policies on the Argentine

00:07:14.541 --> 00:07:18.220
people in an “Open Letter” to the military
regime.

00:07:18.220 --> 00:07:25.330
During this first year of government, consumption
of food has decreased by 40 percent, consumption

00:07:25.330 --> 00:07:32.220
of clothing by more than 50 percent, and the
consumption of medicine is practically at

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:34.710
zero among the lower class.

00:07:34.710 --> 00:07:39.610
“The results” of the policies, he wrote,
“have been devastating.”

00:07:39.610 --> 00:07:48.720
The day after he wrote those words, Walsh
was gunned down in the street by the government’s

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:49.720
soldiers.

00:07:49.720 --> 00:07:52.379
They never returned the body.

00:07:52.379 --> 00:07:57.940
And in both its violent repression and its
economic “reform,” the Argentine government

00:07:57.940 --> 00:08:04.330
had the firm support of the United States
and the International Monetary Fund.

00:08:04.330 --> 00:08:13.569
In 1976, the U.S. gave Argentina $50 million
in “security assistance,” while the IMF

00:08:13.569 --> 00:08:15.400
extended a new loan.

00:08:15.400 --> 00:08:25.060
In 1977, the U.S. provided about $700,000
to train hundreds of Argentine military officers.

00:08:25.060 --> 00:08:34.190
And in 1978, the U.S. sold $120 million in
military parts to Argentina.

00:08:34.190 --> 00:08:39.090
It was only in the middle of President Jimmy
Carter’s term that the United States began

00:08:39.090 --> 00:08:43.310
to question its role in funding the Argentine
dictatorship.

00:08:43.310 --> 00:08:50.761
An American diplomat, Patricia Darien, wrote
a report detailing the extent of the Argentine

00:08:50.761 --> 00:08:52.090
government’s brutality.

00:08:52.090 --> 00:08:53.420
She said:

00:08:53.420 --> 00:09:00.910
The electric picana, something like a supercharged
cattle prod, is still apparently a favorite

00:09:00.910 --> 00:09:07.300
tool [of torture], as is the ‘submarine’
treatment (immersion of the head in a tub

00:09:07.300 --> 00:09:13.170
of water, urine, excrement, blood, or a combination
of these).

00:09:13.170 --> 00:09:15.840
But others in the U.S. did not care.

00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:23.140
A California politician named RONALD REAGAN
responded that Darien should “walk a mile

00:09:23.140 --> 00:09:28.020
in the moccasins” of the Argentine generals
before criticizing them.

00:09:28.020 --> 00:09:34.060
Reagan said that “the incarceration of a
few innocents…is NO REASON to open the jails

00:09:34.060 --> 00:09:37.040
and let the terrorists run free.”

00:09:37.040 --> 00:09:44.330
Kissinger went to Argentina as General Videla’s
personal guest, and praised his so-called

00:09:44.330 --> 00:09:47.520
“efforts in combating terrorism.”

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:53.770
In response to the revelations of torture
and murder, the Carter administration cut

00:09:53.770 --> 00:09:59.000
aid to Argentina—from $30 million to $15
million.

00:09:59.000 --> 00:10:07.880
But after 1979 the U.S. government CONTINUED
to back the Argentines as the leaders of anti-communist

00:10:07.880 --> 00:10:13.970
repression in the Americas, even looking the
other way as they trafficked money through

00:10:13.970 --> 00:10:18.570
the United States to fund death squads in
Central America.

00:10:18.570 --> 00:10:25.570
And when Reagan became president in 1981,
American support became more explicit.

00:10:25.570 --> 00:10:32.530
A leading general, Leopoldo Galtieri, was
invited to Washington, and Reagan sought to

00:10:32.530 --> 00:10:36.070
resume arm sales and loans to Argentina.

00:10:36.070 --> 00:10:43.440
An official in the Reagan Administration named
ELLIOT ABRAMS later admitted that he knew

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:50.310
that the Argentine government was stealing
babies from prisoners and giving them to military

00:10:50.310 --> 00:10:52.630
officers, but did nothing.

00:10:52.630 --> 00:11:00.820
But in 1982, amid a failing economy and growing
protests, the military government decided

00:11:00.820 --> 00:11:08.160
to salvage their popularity by invading the
Malvinas Islands, a territory occupied by

00:11:08.160 --> 00:11:09.160
Britain.

00:11:09.160 --> 00:11:11.630
But they made a big mistake.

00:11:11.630 --> 00:11:17.960
The U.S. was forced to side with Britain,
and Argentina was left isolated.

00:11:17.960 --> 00:11:23.220
The war went terribly, and the Argentines
were quickly defeated.

00:11:23.220 --> 00:11:29.250
With the economic crisis, the brutal repression,
and the failure of the Malvinas War, criticism

00:11:29.250 --> 00:11:32.150
of the military became more open.

00:11:32.150 --> 00:11:38.710
By the end of 1983, the military government
fell, and a new, democratic government was

00:11:38.710 --> 00:11:39.710
elected.

00:11:39.710 --> 00:11:42.370
The new government put the military on trial.

00:11:42.370 --> 00:11:48.800
The JUICIO A LAS JUNTAS—“the trial of
the juntas”—was unique in the region.

00:11:48.800 --> 00:11:56.400
But after the trial ended, right-wing violence
forced the president to set HUNDREDS of torturers

00:11:56.400 --> 00:11:59.650
free, imprisoning only the generals.

00:11:59.650 --> 00:12:07.800
And in the 1990s, President Carlos Menem—a
neoliberal tied closely to the United States—even

00:12:07.800 --> 00:12:10.660
pardoned the generals.

00:12:10.660 --> 00:12:17.310
It was only when leftist governments were
elected in the 2000s that many of the generals

00:12:17.310 --> 00:12:18.930
were imprisoned again.

00:12:18.930 --> 00:12:24.150
In 2010, Videla died in a prison cell in Buenos
Aires.

00:12:24.150 --> 00:12:27.310
Still, the trauma remains fresh.

00:12:27.310 --> 00:12:34.160
The survivors of the prison camps, the families
of the tens of thousands killed, the children

00:12:34.160 --> 00:12:38.480
of the disappeared are alive and with us.

00:12:38.480 --> 00:12:43.500
In Argentina, it remains an open wound—very
alive, and very painful.

00:12:43.500 --> 00:12:47.660
But the story was different in the United
States.

00:12:47.660 --> 00:12:54.330
No one who supported the tortures and murders
in Argentina—from Henry Kissinger to Ronald

00:12:54.330 --> 00:12:55.760
Reagan—was punished.

00:12:55.760 --> 00:13:01.750
Elliot Abrams served in the U.S. government
as recently as 2021.

00:13:01.750 --> 00:13:04.200
EVERYONE got away with it.

00:13:04.200 --> 00:13:07.800
Those wounds, too, remain fresh.

00:13:07.800 --> 00:13:47.510
I’m Pablo Pryluka, PhD student at Princeton
University, for the Gravel Institute.

